Lab 7: Multi-Network Microservices, Volumes, and Bind Mounts
Architecture
[frontend] --(frontend-backend)--> [backend] --(backend-notification)--> [notification]
   :8080                              |                                      |
                                bind mount + logs volume              logs volume
1. Clone the application
bash
git clone https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/MicroserviceApp.git
cd MicroserviceApp
ls   # check the folders of frontend / backend / notification

Build:

bash
docker build -t frontend:v1     ./frontend
docker build -t backend:v1      ./backend
docker build -t notification:v1 ./notification
2. Create two bridge networks with separate subnets
bash
docker network create --driver bridge --subnet 172.20.0.0/24 frontend-backend
docker network create --driver bridge --subnet 172.21.0.0/24 backend-notification
3. Create the volumes
bash
docker volume create backend-logs
docker volume create notification-logs
4. Create the host directory for the bind mount and create env file 
bash
mkdir -p $(pwd)/backend-data
env.txt 
NOTIFICATION_URL=http://notification:5001/notify
BACKEND_URL=http://backend:5000

5. Run the notification service (backend-notification network)
bash
docker run -d --name notification \
  --network backend-notification \
  -v notification-logs:/app/logs \
  notification:v1
6. Run the backend on both networks

A container can only be attached to one network at docker run; connect the second one afterwards.

bash
docker run -d --name backend \
  --network backend-notification \
  -v backend-logs:/app/logs \
  -v $(pwd)/backend-data:/app/data \
  --env-file=env.txt \
  backend:v1                                      
docker network connect frontend-backend backend

Containers resolve each other by name on user-defined networks, so use notification / backend instead of IPs.

7. Run the frontend (frontend-backend network, publish 8080)
bash
docker run -d --name frontend \
  --network frontend-backend \
  -p 8080:80 \
  --env-file=env.txt \
  frontend:v1                                     
8. Verif
bash
docker ps
docker network inspect frontend-backend
docker network inspect backend-notification
docker logs backend
docker logs notification
ls backend-data            
Open http://localhost:8080, create a message, then refresh and confirm it is retrieved.

Cleanup
bash
docker rm -f frontend backend notification
docker network rm frontend-backend backend-notification
docker volume rm backend-logs notification-logs
